markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Why Iteration Matters

Many programs don’t just run one step and stop. They repeat actions over and over—checking for new input, updating a screen, or responding to a user. 

This repeated execution of instructions is called **iteration**, and it’s a core idea behind how web apps and programs work.

Before we look at how iteration appears in code, let’s ground the idea in a familiar situation.

---

<img src="https://images.code.org/94f55e513cce8e0dc448aff4c12c4f33-image-1781706617134.png" alt="basketball going through hoop" width="180px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 60px 0 30px;">


## Hoop, there it is! 


Imagine you are practicing your free throw shot.

**Round 1:**
You take exactly five free throws.

**Round 2:**
You keep tossing the basketball until you make one shot, no matter how many tries it takes.

👉**Which round do you think would take longer and why?**



<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px; width:60%;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em; font-weight: bold;">💡 Check your thinking:</summary>
<div style="margin: 20px;">
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;">
    <ul>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">In one case, you already know how many times you’ll repeat the action.</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 12pt;">In the other, you don’t know how many tries it will take—you stop only when a condition is met.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style = "font-size: 12pt;"> This same distinction shows up in programming.</p>

</p>
</div>
</details>

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

---

## What is Iteration?

Instead of writing the same instructions over and over, programmers use loops to tell the computer how and when to repeat steps.

<div style="background-color: #e4e2f8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 80%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 <strong>Iteration</strong> is the process of repeating a set of steps in a program.
</p>
</div>

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

<img src="https://images.code.org/c4bb7cf4e168492738989f3ac3a13c5e-image-1781707167171.png" alt="icon of looping arrows" width="210px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: -25px 0px 0px -20px;">

**In web apps and other programs, iteration is used to:**

- Check for user input repeatedly

- Update information on the screen

- Process lists of data

- Keep a program running until something changes



<div style="clear:both;"></div>

